# Reuse existing functionality

Before creating **new** functionality or updating an **existing** one, **search the codebase first** for the same (or close) behavior and prefer a shared implementation over a one-off copy.

## Required scan (before writing code)

1. Search by behavior name, types, helpers, hooks, components, services, and similar call sites (`grep`, semantic search, and/or explore subagents).
2. Check likely shared homes first: `shared/`, domain packages, existing hooks/utils, theme/design-system helpers, and nearby feature modules that already do the same job.
3. Prefer, in order: **reuse as-is** → **extend the shared module** → **extract shared** only when two+ call sites need the same logic → **local one-off** only when nothing fits.

## Rules

- ❌ Do **not** reimplement helpers, hooks, UI patterns, validation, date/range logic, API mappers, or domain rules that already exist elsewhere
- ❌ Do **not** copy-paste a near-duplicate into a second feature file when a shared module can own it
- ✅ If a close match exists but is incomplete, **extend that shared place** (or extract one) instead of forking
- ✅ If reuse would force an awkward abstraction, stop and ask — do not invent a “shared” API that only one caller needs
- ✅ This applies to both **greenfield** work and **updates** to existing features (an update is a chance to converge on shared code)

## Quick check

Ask: “Would another screen/service need this same behavior?” If yes (or if it already exists twice), put it in shared code and call that.
